\section{Pay for combinations, not just individual rules}

The cheapest rule at a producer may leave a value in a form that is expensive for its consumers. Eckstein, K\"onig and Scholz's \emph{Code Instruction Selection based on SSA-Graphs} makes that problem concrete using a DSP whose fixed-point multiply leaves a shifted result. Keeping a loop-carried value in the internal mode can avoid shifts in the body, while the return still needs the ordinary representation. Hjort Blindell describes their selection model as a partitioned Boolean quadratic problem (PBQP): pick one rule per SSA node and pay additional costs for pairs of rules connected by def-use edges \cite[chapter~5]{blindell2016instruction}.

Give each decision \(v\) a domain of available alternatives and a vector \(c_v\) of unary costs. One alternative \(x_v\) must be selected. A table \(C_{uv}\), with rows for \(u\) and columns for \(v\), gives the cost of each combination. The objective is
\[
  \min_x \left(\sum_v c_v[x_v] +
                  \sum_{(u,v)} C_{uv}[x_u,x_v]\right).
\]
This notation looks up the entries directly; the usual quadratic expression uses one-hot Boolean vectors. Two alternatives that agree on the interface can have a zero pair cost. A required conversion contributes its cost; an impossible combination is forbidden, conventionally written \(+\infty\). Where several chain rules can translate the produced mode into the required one, compute the least legal chain cost and retain the sequence for emission. If an edge-specific conversion cannot actually be placed, its matrix entry must be forbidden, not merely expensive. When two flows contribute costs between the same decisions, add their tables after aligning their rows and columns. This assumes distinct charges; if one conversion serves both flows, counting it twice would bias the model.

