\section{Choose a loop representation and count edge traffic}

Consider a toy machine with a general register class \(G\) and a fast accumulator class \(A\). Both hold the same 32-bit value exactly, and the two alternatives for a recurrence step compute the same wrapping result. Let \(p\) supply the initial value, \(h\) be the loop-header \(\phi\), \(u\) the step in the loop body, and \(r\) the return. On the modeled workload the step executes eight times, each time followed by a backedge to \(h\); there is also one entry and one exit. Unary costs for \(p\) are \(1\) in \(G\) and \(2\) in \(A\). The step costs \(3\) in \(G\), \(1\) in \(A\), per execution. The \(\phi\) has zero base cost, and the return has only a zero-cost \(G\) alternative. A class change costs two per edge traversal. Assume conversion placements are legal on all the given edges.

\Cref{fig:visual-277} puts the cost of a bank change on the edge that actually carries the converted value.

