\begin{verbatim}
# Charge each mismatch at the frequency of its actual edge.
p -> h: 2;     h -> u: 16;   u -> h: 16;   h -> r: 2
u unary: G=24, A=8.  Matching classes cost 0 on every edge.

# For each (h,u), minimize p first; r is fixed to G.
 (h,u)     best p       minimum total
 (G,G)       G          1 + 24          = 25
 (G,A)       G          1 +  8 + 32     = 41
 (A,G)       A          2 + 24 + 32 + 2 = 60
 (A,A)       A          2 +  8 +  2     = 12
\end{verbatim}

Thus the least-cost solution keeps the recurrence in \(A\) and copies to \(G\) once on the way out. If the target has no legal \(A\)-to-\(G\) transfer, replace the corresponding entries by \(+\infty\). The choices with \(h\) in \(A\) then fail at the return and the choice with \(u\) alone in \(A\) fails on the backedge. The all-\(G\) assignment remains feasible at 25. The invariant is that every finite-cost edge admits a concrete implementation with equal values on the two sides of the interface. Counting cheapest defaults while discarding the incompatible edges would not preserve that invariant.

\section{Reduce what is exact; identify what is not}

The original SSA-graph paper describes a reduction phase followed by back-propagation. For example, a decision \(v\) interacting only with \(u\) can be removed: for each alternative of \(u\), add the minimum of \(c_v+C_{uv}\) over alternatives of \(v\) to \(u\)'s unary cost. Retain the minimizing choice so it can be restored. This is exactly the calculation for \(p\) in the table. A degree-two elimination can similarly induce a new pair table. Higher-degree elimination may induce a cost over more than two remaining decisions, so general PBQP is not just tree dynamic programming. If a heuristic fixes one of those decisions, verify the assignment and report it as feasible rather than proven optimal. Recover with a verified baseline if a bounded search fails to provide a legal cover.

\section{Tie a multi-operation choice together}

With several covered roots, independent per-node choices can select half an instruction. Hjort Blindell describes Ebner et al.'s extension using complex-rule instances and local proxy choices \cite[chapter~5]{blindell2016instruction}. In a simple implementation with instance-specific proxies, give each complex match an on/off decision. Pairwise constraints force every participating node to choose that match's proxy when it is on, and forbid those proxies when it is off. If proxies have zero base cost, charge the actual instruction once at the on decision. Single coverage of participating nodes then excludes overlapping matches. It does not guarantee schedulability: collapsing operation groups can create a dependency cycle, potentially involving more than two selected matches. Check the resulting dependencies and order, distinguishing genuine loop-carried edges from impossible same-iteration cycles. A minimum PBQP score is only a minimum for the decisions, compatibility constraints, and costs represented in the model.
